{
  "beruf": "kita",
  "title": "Cleaning and childcare",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "french-for-work run, 2026-11-13: written by hand for France, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Cleaning (in offices or in private homes) and looking after children are common first jobs for newcomers in France, often part time or with split hours. This deck covers products and tools, what you do, the working day, safety and health, and how to talk to parents, employers and the team. Use vous with parents and employers; colleagues often say tu.",
  "introEasy": "Do you clean or look after children in France? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Agent d'entretien / agente d'entretien",
    "Agent de propreté",
    "Aide ménager / aide ménagère",
    "Assistant maternel agréé / assistante maternelle agréée",
    "Garde d'enfants à domicile",
    "Auxiliaire de puériculture"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Ask before you start in a new place: « Où est le matériel de ménage, s'il vous plaît ? » and « Quels produits est-ce que je peux utiliser ? » Never use a product you do not know.",
    "With parents and employers say vous and use their name: « Bonjour madame Martin, Léo a bien dormi. » Colleagues in a cleaning team often say tu.",
    "Tell the parents the important things at pick-up: « Il a eu de la fièvre cet après-midi. » Write down medicine, sleep and meals if the family asks for it.",
    "Rules for childminders, CESU or Pajemploi payment, and cleaning contracts depend on the job and the employer. Ask the employer, France Travail, the PMI or a union. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
